{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `agree` - the assertion that cannot be regenerated - Design-Test.md §8.
   ==========================================================================================

   Several independent ways of getting an answer, asserted to get the *same* answer. There is no
   expected value here, no golden file and nothing to update, and that is the whole point:
   **neither side of the assertion is a file**, so no regeneration can quietly make it agree with a
   bad pass. Every other assertion in this library compares something a test computed against
   something a person wrote down, and a person who writes the wrong thing down twice has a green
   suite.

   It is the shape of most of the strong assertions this repository already makes - a `moveMemory`
   against a byte loop, `crc32` the instruction against `crc32` the table, a `Map` against an
   association list - and of several it does not, which §8 tabulates. Those are all *functions*, and
   they are what this file covers. The other half of §8's table is separate builds - specialized
   against forced-generic, amd64 against JavaScript - and those ways are the harness's rather than
   the runner's: the `Way` list comes from a matrix outside the binary, and what runs here is the
   same comparison over the answers it collected.

   **The report names the odd one out, not the failure.** "three ways, two agreed - `js` answered 17,
   `native` and `native-noopt` answered 16" is a sentence somebody can act on; "agreement failed" is
   not. The groups are printed smallest first for exactly that reason - the minority is the thing
   being looked for, and putting it at the front means it is the first thing read.
-}

{-
   One way of getting the answer.

   `() -> String` rather than a type variable, which is §8's own shape and is a deliberate narrowing.
   Two ways of computing something may not agree on a *type* - a `Map`'s contents and an association
   list's are two different types holding the same information - and text is the one thing every
   answer can be reduced to. It also makes the report printable without a `Show` constraint that
   half the interesting cases could not satisfy.
-}
pub data Way {name: String, run: () -> String}

pub fn agree(label: String, ways: [Way], @caller ->at: Site) -> {}:
    match disagreementOf(label, ways):
        Nothing -> {}
        Just(->claim) -> record(Failure {site: at, claim: claim, detail: ""})

pub fn requireAgree(label: String, ways: [Way], @caller ->at: Site) -> Test:
    match disagreementOf(label, ways):
        Nothing -> return Ok({})
        Just(->claim) -> return failing(Failure {site: at, claim: claim, detail: ""})

{-
   Every way run, and the answers grouped.

   **Fewer than two ways is a failure rather than a pass**, which is `test/README.md`'s empty-corpus
   guard in the one place it is easiest to trip over: a matrix that produced a single build, or a
   list a `if` emptied, is an `agree` that holds vacuously. One way agreeing with itself asserts
   nothing, and something that asserts nothing must not be mistaken for something that asserts
   everything.

   Grouping is by first appearance rather than by a hash, so it needs no `Hash(String)` and the
   order the report comes out in is the order the ways were written in. O(n^2) over the number of
   *ways*, which is three.

   **Public for the reason `snapshotOutcome` is.** A test of what a disagreement reports cannot be
   written in terms of `agree`, because the assertion would record the failure it was checking for.
   The predicate and the `record` are separable in every assertion here; in the two the framework
   tests itself with, the split is reachable.
-}
pub fn disagreementOf(label: String, ways: [Way]) -> Maybe(String):
    let count = length(ways)

    if count < (2 :: Size):
        return Just("{label}: agree needs at least two ways and got {count} - one way agreeing with itself asserts nothing")

    let &answers = [] :: [String]
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < count:
        let way = ways[i]
        push(answers, way.run())
        i = i + 1

    -- Each way's group, named by the first way that gave the same answer. A way that is its own
    -- leader is a group, so the leaders are the distinct answers and nothing has to be deduplicated.
    let &leader = [] :: [Size]
    let &j = 0 :: Size

    while j < count:
        let &found = j
        let &k = 0 :: Size

        while k < j:
            if answers[k] == answers[j]:
                found = k
                k = j
            else:
                k = k + 1

        push(leader, found)
        j = j + 1

    let &groups = 0 :: Size
    let &largest = 0 :: Size
    let &g = 0 :: Size

    while g < count:
        if leader[g] == g:
            groups = groups + 1
            let size = groupSize(leader, g)
            if size > largest then largest = size

        g = g + 1

    if groups <= (1 :: Size) then return Nothing

    -- Smallest group first, so the odd one out is the first thing in the sentence. Ties keep the
    -- order the ways were written in, which is what makes the line the same on every run.
    let &text = "{label}: {count} ways, {largest} agreed"
    let &size = 1 :: Size

    while size <= count:
        let &at = 0 :: Size

        while at < count:
            if leader[at] == at && groupSize(leader, at) == size:
                text ++= " - {namesOfGroup(ways, leader, at)} answered {answers[at]}"

            at = at + 1

        size = size + 1

    return Just(text)

fn groupSize(leader: [Size], of: Size) -> Size:
    let &size = 0 :: Size
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(leader):
        if leader[i] == of then size = size + 1
        i = i + 1

    return size

-- `a`, `a and b`, `a, b and c` - the English form rather than a comma list, because the line is read
-- as a sentence and "`native`, `native-noopt` answered 16" is not one.
fn namesOfGroup(ways: [Way], leader: [Size], of: Size) -> String:
    let size = groupSize(leader, of)
    let &text = "" :: String
    let &shown = 0 :: Size
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(leader):
        if leader[i] == of:
            if shown > (0 :: Size):
                if shown + 1 == size then text ++= " and " else text ++= ", "

            text ++= "`{ways[i].name}`"
            shown = shown + 1

        i = i + 1

    return text
